[{:adr/authoritative_for ["cloud-itonami app-mangaka / app-animeka clj twin inference boundary" "status of ADR-2605215000 as authority in cloud-itonami code" "separation of the model-pin violation from the allowlist-shape question"], :adr/topic "allowlist-blocks-the-alias-it-cites", :adr/related ["adr-2607173100-fleet-main-model-switchability" "2605215000" "2606172359"], :adr/status "proposed", :adr/id "adr-2608123800-allowlist-blocks-the-alias-it-cites", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/last_verified "2026-08-12", :adr/body "# ADR-2608123800: the allowlist that blocks the alias cites the ADR that repealed it\n\n**Status**: proposed — three of the four sub-questions are settled by evidence below; the\nfourth needs an owner decision and is stated as such in §8.\n**Date**: 2026-08-12\n**Decider**: Jun Kawasaki\n\n## What was asked\n\nThree `cloud-itonami/app-*` repos were fixed (merges `57b1299e`, `fa78cae3`, `65c0f15d`)\nto stop defaulting inference at a released RunPod pod id and to stop pinning a concrete\nmodel id, per ADR-2607173100. Their Clojure twins were not fixed, and look unfixable\nwithout reversing an accepted ADR:\n\n- `app-mangaka/lg-clj/src/lg_mangaka/llm.cljc` pins `:model \"gemma3:4b\"`;\n  `app-animeka/lg-clj/src/lg_animeka/llm.cljc` pins `:model \"tier0-general\"`.\n- Both carry `assert-murakumo`, an allowlist that admits only a fixed host set and only\n  the `http` scheme, which structurally rejects `https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1`.\n- Both cite ADR-2605215000 and ADR-2606172359 as the authority for that allowlist.\n\nThis document establishes what the four documents require, what the allowlist actually\nprotects, what each way out costs, and which part still needs the owner. It implements\nnothing.\n\n## 1. What the documents actually say\n\n### ADR-2607173100 — accepted, authoritative\n\nIt forbids the pin and fixes the resolution order:\n\n> concrete な model id をコード・routine・設定既定値に焼くことを禁止\n\n> 解決順の規約: env/引数 override → alias 解決 → fallback は endpoint のみ焼く\n> (endpoint 先の serving モデルに従う。model 名は焼かない)\n\nIts deciders line records the owner directive it rests on: 「gemma は deprecated で ok,\n3.6 main で. model はすぐに能力が変わるので, 切り替えられるように設計して, repo wide に.」\n\n### ADR-2605215000 — superseded, and not authoritative\n\nThis is the finding that changes the shape of the question. The file's own front matter\nreads `:adr/status \"superseded\"` and `:adr/authoritative false`, with `:adr/superseded_by`\npointing at 2606172359. Its body opens by saying so:\n\n> **SUPERSEDED 2026-06-17 by ADR-2606172359.** The categorical \"Murakumo-only / no\n> commercial GPU\" invariant is repealed. [...] the Murakumo fleet remains the DEFAULT and\n> PREFERRED path (high-scoring), but it is no longer a hard ban.\n\nNote also what it required while it *was* live, because the twins do not implement it.\nIts §1.1 authoritative stack is LiteLLM gateway at `http://192.168.1.70:4000` (LAN, bearer\nkey), EVO-X2 Ollama at `192.168.1.70:11434`, ComfyUI at `192.168.1.70:8188`, and a\nper-node Ollama `gemma3:4b` at `http://127.0.0.1:11434` described as the per-node\n*fallback*. Its §1.2 forbidden list is a list of commercial vendors: `api.runpod.ai`,\n`*.proxy.runpod.net`, OpenAI direct, Anthropic direct, Vertex AI, AWS Bedrock, Lambda\nLabs, CoreWeave, Vast.ai.\n\n**The invariant was never \"loopback only\" and never \"http only\".** It was \"no commercial\nGPU rental\" — a sovereignty rule about vendor dependency, argued that way throughout\n(alternative A2 is rejected on the grounds that \"the objection is not to RunPod\nspecifically but to the category commercial GPU rental\"). Every endpoint in its own table\nhappens to be plain `http` on a private network, so the twins' `http`-only scheme check is\na *consequence* of transcribing that endpoint list, not a decision to forbid TLS. No text\nin either ADR forbids `https`.\n\n### ADR-2606172359 — the ADR that repealed the rule, cited by the twins in its defence\n\nThe twins name this ADR as co-authority for the allowlist. It is the document that\nabolished the thing the allowlist enforces. D1:\n\n> 商用 GPU inference の **categorical 禁止を撤廃**する [...] **Murakumo fleet は引き続き\n> DEFAULT かつ preferred**(透明・自律・低 lock-in ゆえ高スコア)。だが「Murakumo 以外＝即\n> Non-Aligned」という固定ルールは廃止。[...] ADR-2605215000 は **superseded**。\n\nD5 then names the work that was left undone, and the twins are in scope of it:\n\n> 残る sweep(follow-up ADR で実施): §2(i) を参照する ~30 actor ADR / lint hook\n> (`lint-dangerous-query` 系の commercial-GPU gate)。\n\nSo the allowlist is not a standing invariant that ADR-2607173100 collides with. It is a\nknown, acknowledged, un-executed item of downstream debt, recorded as such by the ADR the\ntwins cite.\n\nIts `:adr/status` still reads `\"proposed\"`, which would ordinarily leave ratification\nopen. It does not here: `CHARTER-RIDER.md` records the amendment as applied and has moved\ntwice since —\n\n> Supersedes (v3.3): v3.2 (2026-06-16) per ADR-2606172359 — §2(d) and §2(i) reframed:\n> from categorical FIXED RULES to NET-EFFECT evaluation [...] §2(i): Murakumo-only\n> inference is no longer a categorical ban — commercial compute is evaluated by lock-in /\n> transparency / carbon (Murakumo stays the default-preferred path).\n\nand the rider is at v3.5 today. The stale status field is a documentation defect worth\nfixing separately; it is not a live question about whether the repeal took effect.\n\n### CLAUDE.md, LLM model selection section\n\nRepo-wide mandatory, and the binding text for new work: 「モデルは能力がすぐ入れ替わる。\nconcrete な model id をコード・スクリプト・routine prompt・設定の既定値にハードコード\nしない。」 with the same three-step resolution order, and the note that gemma4-26b is\ndeprecated by owner instruction.\n\n## 2. Why the allowlist exists, taken seriously\n\nIt would be wrong to read `assert-murakumo` as an accident, and wrong to delete it just\nbecause its citation is dead. It was doing real work, and the twins say what:\n\n> DEVIATION (noted): the Python posts to a RunPod-served vLLM proxy URL. Per\n> ADR-2605215000 / 2606172359 [...] the LLM edge here defaults to the Murakumo loopback\n> gateway [...] and asserts the endpoint is on the Murakumo fleet allowlist.\n\nThat is an honest and well-constructed guard. These files are ports of Python whose\ndefault endpoint really was `https://vyp99t9px7h4dl-4000.proxy.runpod.net/v1` — a\ncommercial GPU rental host, and the exact category §1.2 forbids. The port author could not\nfix the Python, so they contained the port: a deny-by-default allowlist, applied at the\ncall boundary, that makes the forbidden endpoint *unrepresentable* rather than merely\ndiscouraged. It fails closed, throws rather than warns, and carries the ADR id in the\nerror payload. If the question were only \"does this control protect something real\", the\nanswer would be yes.\n\nTwo qualifications, so the control is not credited with more than it has.\n\n**It does not implement the ADR it cites.** §1.1's LiteLLM gateway is\n`192.168.1.70:4000`; the twins default to `127.0.0.1:4000`, which is not in the table.\nThe allowlist admits `192.168.1.70:8077`, which is not an inference endpoint at all — a\nmigration-plan document in `etzhayyim/root` identifies `:8077` as the kotoba node port.\nSo the host set is an approximation of §1.1, not a transcription of it.\n\n**Its scheme check inverts the property it protects.** The rule permits only `http` and\nonly these hosts. The effect today is that the *unencrypted* paths are the permitted ones\nand every *encrypted* path is refused. Against the RunPod threat that is incidental —\nRunPod happened to be https — but it means the guard cannot be widened by adding a host\nwithout also relaxing the scheme, and that the current shape actively prefers cleartext.\n\n## 3. What measurement changed\n\nThe threat model this guard was built against has been retired at its source, and the\nguard's own defaults have gone dead. Measured 2026-08-12 from this workstation:\n\n| Probe | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| `GET https://api.murakumo.cloud/infer/models/murakumo-main` | **200** — `alias-for: qwen3.6-35b-a3b`, `endpoint: https://infer.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions`, `status: serving`, `vision: true` |\n| `http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1/models` — the twins' default endpoint | **connection failed** (no LiteLLM on this box) |\n| `http://192.168.1.70:4000/v1/models` — §1.1's gateway | **connection failed from here** |\n| `http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/tags` — per-node Ollama | **200**, serving `qwen3.6:35b-a3b-coding-nvfp4`, `qwen3.6:27b-coding-nvfp4`, `gemma4:26b-a4b-it-qat`, `gemma4:12b-it-qat`, `gemma4:e4b`, `gemma4:e2b`, `gemma4:latest` |\n| `gemma3:4b` present on that Ollama | **absent** |\n\nRead carefully, because one row is weaker than it looks: this workstation is not\nnecessarily on the `192.168.1.70` LAN, so that row shows unreachability *from here* and\ndoes not prove the EVO-X2 gateway is down. The loopback rows are stronger — loopback is\nloopback wherever you run it.\n\nThe load-bearing rows are the last two. The mangaka twin pins `gemma3:4b`, and the only\nallowlisted endpoint that is actually up does not serve it. The pin is dead against the\none host that could have honoured it.\n\nAnd the Python that these files were containing has been fixed. `20fb32d` in app-mangaka\nmoved `VLLM_URL` from the RunPod proxy to `https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1` and `VLLM_MODEL`\nfrom `tier0-general` to `murakumo-main`, recording that the RunPod pod is already dead\n(404 on both paths) and that the exposure was latent rather than active. **The endpoint the\nallowlist exists to forbid is no longer the endpoint the other side of the port uses.** The\nallowlist now blocks the fleet and permits nothing that is running.\n\n## 4. Are the twins live?\n\nNo. Both are dormant ports, and one of them is structurally dead. This matters because it\nsets the urgency of everything above to near zero, and because it makes option E cheap.\n\n**Neither `lg-clj/` tree has been modified since the day it was copied in.** In both repos\n`git log -- lg-clj/` returns exactly one commit — the extraction (`ec2342a` / `55ad55a`,\n2026-07-20). Meanwhile `git log -- lg/` in both repos includes today's alias fix. The only\nsubstantive post-extraction code commit in either repo went to Python and skipped the twin.\n\n**Nothing builds, gates or deploys them.** `scripts/fleet-ci/gates.edn` and\n`manifest/fleet-ci.edn` contain zero references to either app. Neither repo has a\n`.github/` directory, a Makefile, a `wrangler.toml`, or any deploy script touching\n`lg-clj`. The only Dockerfiles are Python (`CMD [\"uvicorn\", \"lg_animeka.server:app\", …]`).\nThe twins are reachable only by hand, from inside `lg-clj/`, through a `bb.edn` — a\nbabashka entry point, on the script host CLAUDE.md is actively retiring. Both repos are\nwest-registered, but registration is not a gate.\n\n**mangaka is not merely dormant, it cannot run.** Three independent blocks: `-main` throws\n`Mangaka portable runtime requires an explicit server host adapter` unconditionally, and\nthe `bb serve` task calls exactly that; `handler` passes an empty capability map so\n`*http-post*` is `nil`; and `chat` throws when `*http-post*` is not a fn. `run-server-with`,\nthe one function that could inject a real client, is called from nowhere in the repo, and\nno host adapter file exists. Outside test stubs, `*http-post*` is bound to a real HTTP\nclient in zero places. `llm-json` has no caller at all.\n\n**animeka is dormant but not dead**, and the difference is worth preserving in the record.\nIt has a real host adapter — `lg-clj/run_tests.clj`, ns `lg-animeka.host`, binding\n`llm/*chat*` to a `babashka.http-client/post` call through `chat-with` — and ten genuine\ngraph-node consumers calling `llm/chat` in node bodies. It could be started by hand. Nothing\nstarts it.\n\nThe clearest statement of intent is in the mangaka twin's own `server.cljc` docstring:\n\n> The Python FastAPI server under `lg/` stays the deployed runtime and COEXISTS; this twin\n> is additive (py_removed=0).\n\nThe twin declares itself additive to a Python runtime that is deployed. It never claimed to\nbe the authority, and the drift measured in §3 is what happens to an additive copy when the\nauthority moves.\n\nOne caveat I could not close: I could not confirm from this workspace whether *either*\napp's LangGraph backend is deployed anywhere at all. Animeka's Python tree is itself\nincomplete here — `lg/langgraph.json` declares 27 graphs but only 6 of the `.py` files are\npresent, and `lg/lg_animeka/server.py`, which its own Dockerfile invokes, has never existed\nin this repo's history. So \"Python is live\" is well-evidenced for mangaka and only\npartially evidenced for animeka. Neither reading makes the clj twins live.\n\n## 5. The two violations are separate, and only one of them is contested\n\nThe brief treats this as one conflict. It is two, with different difficulty.\n\n**The model pin is wrong under every document, including the superseded ones.** Nothing\nrequires these particular strings:\n\n- ADR-2605215000 mentions `gemma3:4b` once, as the per-node loopback *fallback* row of\n  §1.1. It is a description of what the fleet ran, not a requirement to pin it in source.\n- ADR-2606172359 says nothing about model ids at all; it is about compute sourcing.\n- ADR-2607173100 and CLAUDE.md forbid the pin outright, and gemma is deprecated by owner\n  directive.\n- `tier0-general` is a LiteLLM routing tier rather than a raw weights name, so it is the\n  milder of the two — but this workspace has already ruled on that exact string: `20fb32d`\n  removed it from the Python calling it \"a concrete model id\" that \"ADR-2607173100 forbids\n  pinning\".\n\nSo the pin can be removed with no ADR reversed and no control lost. **It is not part of\nthe conflict.** It is only entangled with the allowlist because both live in the same\n`default-config` map.\n\n**The allowlist shape is the contested half**, and it is contested against a repealed\nrule rather than a live one. That is the part §7 and §8 are about.\n\n## 6. Options, and what each actually costs\n\nOrdered by how much they change. Costs assume the liveness finding in §4.\n\n**A. Drop the model pin only; leave the allowlist untouched.**\nRemoves the deprecated-gemma violation and the `tier0-general` violation. Nothing breaks:\nthe model is dead on the only reachable allowlisted host anyway, and callers that bind\n`host-config` keep overriding as before. Loses no control — the allowlist is orthogonal to\nthe model string. Requires nobody to change anything. **This is settled on the evidence and\ndoes not need a decision.** The only design question is what replaces the pin: with the\nendpoint still loopback-only, `murakumo-main` would be a name that host cannot resolve, so\nthe honest replacement is to leave the model unset and require the caller to supply it,\nwhich is the shape `app-dougaka` already chose (see D).\n\n**B. Widen the allowlist to admit the fleet over https, keep it otherwise.**\nAdd `api.murakumo.cloud` / `infer.murakumo.cloud` and permit `https` for them. Preserves\nthe anti-commercial-rental property — `*.proxy.runpod.net` stays unrepresentable — while\nletting the twins reach the endpoint ADR-2607173100 points at, and stops the guard\npreferring cleartext. Cost: the fleet's public hostnames sit behind a public DNS and CDN\nedge, which is a different and smaller dependency than RunPod but not zero, and someone\nmust decide it is acceptable. This is the smallest change that resolves the stated\nconflict.\n\n**C. Route through the alias and delete `assert-murakumo` entirely.**\nMatches what the Python now does and is the least code. Cost: discards a working\ndeny-by-default control that still makes commercial GPU rental unrepresentable in these\nfiles. ADR-2606172359 did *not* say endpoint choice stops mattering — it said it moves from\na fixed rule to objective-function assessment on lock-in, transparency and carbon. Deleting\nthe check leaves nothing implementing that assessment at this boundary. Choose this only\nif the assessment is genuinely held somewhere else.\n\n**D. Follow `app-dougaka`: unset the defaults, require the host to inject.**\nThere is a precedent in this exact wave. `b803820` declined to route dougaka's speech and\nimage endpoints through `murakumo-main` and set them to `\"\"` instead, on the reasoning that\nthe alias is a chat entry that \"says nothing about speech synthesis or image generation\",\nand that a URL which \"resolves but cannot serve [...] is worse than an obviously-empty\ndefault because it looks configured\". The same argument applies to a loopback default that\nis not running. Cost: any caller relying on the baked default gets an explicit failure\ninstead of a silent one — which is the point, but it is a behaviour change.\n\n**E. Retire the twins.**\n§4 makes this cheaper than it looks. Nothing is on a classpath outside `lg-clj`, nothing\ngates them, nothing deploys them, and mangaka's cannot execute at all. Removing them\nremoves both violations permanently and ends the drift rather than re-synchronising it once.\nThe asymmetry matters: for mangaka this deletes code that provably cannot run; for animeka\nit deletes a working host adapter and ten graph nodes that someone wrote deliberately and\ncould start by hand. Cost: it is the one option that is not cheaply reversible, and it\nforecloses a port whose intent I could not establish — the twins were copied in three weeks\nago and have simply not been touched since, which is consistent with both *abandoned* and\n*not yet resumed*. Note also that whatever is decided, the twins currently depend on a\n`bb.edn` entry point, and CLAUDE.md is retiring babashka as a script host; finishing the\nport means porting the harness too.\n\nB, C, D and E are alternatives for the same half of the problem; A is orthogonal and\ncompatible with all of them. Because §4 shows nothing is running, none of these is urgent —\nthere is no live exposure here. The exposure that existed was in the Python, and it has\nbeen fixed.\n\n## 7. What I could not determine\n\n- **Whether these repos are still governed by the etzhayyim charter.** Both twins were\n  extracted out of `etzhayyim/root` (`ec2342a`, `Extract dougaka from etzhayyim/root`) into\n  the `cloud-itonami` org, and their ADR citations came with them. ADR-2605215000 and\n  ADR-2606172359 are etzhayyim *constitutional* documents — Rider §2(i), Council\n  ratification, Tier-0 priorities. Whether a repo that has moved org still inherits the\n  charter of the org it left is a governance question, and I found nothing in either repo\n  or in the manifest that answers it.\n- **Whether the objective-function assessment ADR-2606172359 substitutes for the ban is\n  implemented anywhere for these repos.** It names ECL scoring dimensions and fixtures in\n  `etzhayyim/root`. I did not establish whether any of that binds a `cloud-itonami` app, or\n  whether at this boundary the repeal simply left a gap.\n- **Why `192.168.1.70:8077` is in an inference allowlist.** The best evidence says `:8077`\n  is the kotoba node port. I could not find the change that added it or a rationale for it.\n- **Whether `192.168.1.70` is reachable in production.** Unreachable from this workstation,\n  which proves nothing about the fleet's own network.\n- **Whether the clj port is still intended.** This is the gap that blocks a recommendation.\n  Untouched-since-extraction is equally consistent with abandoned and with not-yet-resumed,\n  and I found no roadmap, issue, ADR or manifest entry stating which. Animeka's host adapter\n  and ten graph nodes are real work by someone with a plan I cannot read off the tree.\n- **Whether either LangGraph backend is deployed at all.** No deploy manifest in this\n  workspace answers it, and animeka's Python tree is missing the `server.py` its own\n  Dockerfile invokes. I did not run either test suite, so I can say the tests exist and\n  resolve their dependencies, not that they pass.\n\n## 8. The decision requested\n\nThree sub-questions are answered by the evidence above and need no ruling:\n\n1. ADR-2605215000 does not bind — it is marked superseded and non-authoritative in its own\n   front matter, and the charter clause it derives from has been amended three times since.\n2. ADR-2606172359 does not support the allowlist — it is the document that repealed the\n   categorical ban and that logged this exact sweep as unfinished.\n3. The model pins violate every applicable document and can be removed without reversing\n   anything. Option A.\n\nThere is also no live exposure to weigh against them: §4 establishes that nothing runs this\ncode, so the conflict is a documentation and hygiene problem, not an incident.\n\n**The fourth needs the owner**, and it is one question with two halves that have to be\nanswered in order:\n\n> **Is the Clojure port still intended — and if it is, should a Murakumo-preference control\n> remain at this boundary in `cloud-itonami` code, in what shape?**\n\nIf the port is not intended, option E settles everything at once and the allowlist question\nnever has to be answered. If it is intended, the shape question becomes real: a widened host\nallowlist that keeps commercial GPU rental unrepresentable (B), a deny-list naming the\nforbidden category rather than an allow-list naming hosts, or nothing at this layer because\nADR-2606172359 moved the assessment into the objective function (C), with D as the honest\ndefault for the endpoint in the meantime.\n\nI have not recommended between B, C, D and E, and I do not think the evidence supports one.\nThe choice turns on the two things §7 could not establish: whether anyone still intends to\nfinish this port, and whether repos extracted out of `etzhayyim/root` into `cloud-itonami`\nstill inherit the etzhayyim charter that the control derives from. If they do, the control\nbelongs somewhere and B is right; if they answer to `cloud-itonami` alone, it is a fossil of\na former owner and C, D or E is right. Both are questions about intent and governance rather\nthan about the code, and the code cannot answer either.\n\nWhat can be said without the ruling: the twins should stop citing ADR-2605215000 as\nauthority in their docstrings and error payloads whatever else is decided, because that\ncitation is false today and is the reason this looked like an ADR conflict rather than an\nunfinished sweep.\n", :adr/deciders ["Jun Kawasaki"], :adr/date "2026-08-12", :db/id -1, :adr/authoritative true, :adr/title "ADR-2608123800: the allowlist that blocks the alias cites the ADR that repealed it", :adr/source-format "edn"}]
